{
  "abstract": "A machine-checked proof of the formula of Kisielewicz (1988) that writes the Dedekind number M(n), the number of monotone Boolean functions of n variables, as a finite sum of products of binary digits. The formula is proved in two forms: as Formal Conjectures states it, with natural-number arithmetic, and as it is usually printed, with integer arithmetic, digits given by the floor function, and the sum over k from 1 to 2^(2^n). The proof reads k as a family of subsets of an n-element set: the subset coded by the binary digits of i belongs to the family when the i-th digit of k is 1. The innermost product is 1 when the subset coded by i is contained in the subset coded by j, and 0 otherwise. Containment forces i <= j, so only pairs i < j matter: the term of k is 1 when the family is an antichain and 0 otherwise, and the sum counts antichains, which are equinumerous with monotone Boolean functions. The formula is an identity and not a practical algorithm: the sum has 2^(2^n) terms, and no new Dedekind number is computed. The mathematics is classical and no mathematical priority is claimed.",
